{"records":[{"id":"dw3v4sm9nb","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-Blank-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:26:36.327Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dw3v4sm9nb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dw3v4sm9nb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2tv7ntcgh6","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Blank-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:24:02.941Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2tv7ntcgh6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2tv7ntcgh6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dmyfkn7pnz","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-Blank-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:25:54.448Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dmyfkn7pnz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dmyfkn7pnz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75dyzmvbrz","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Sample1-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:30:27.715Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75dyzmvbrz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75dyzmvbrz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4zj3kz34c5","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-analysis results","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:24:31.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4zj3kz34c5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4zj3kz34c5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k9sm56gj5y","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Blank-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:23:56.067Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k9sm56gj5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k9sm56gj5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s7jbxvwf4z","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-L-R2","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:31:49.088Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s7jbxvwf4z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s7jbxvwf4z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fv6f9rpg5m","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 2-L-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:32:38.387Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fv6f9rpg5m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fv6f9rpg5m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xb6s7vzcwm","title":"cisplatin-damaged gene locus-replicate 1-Sample1-R1","description":"Cisplatin is a DNA-targeting chemotherapeutic, yet its damaged gene loci are largely unexplored. We utilized a forward chemical genetics strategy to map 7578 cisplatin-damaged genes (CDGs) with >12 fold-enrichment from A549 human lung cancer cells, of which the highly associated signalling pathways include sperm motility, molecular mechanism of cancer, and protein kinase A signalling. The three most frequently cisplatinated penta-nucleotide motifs and genomic region are CTGGG, CCAGG and GCTGG, and promoter, respectively. Among the CDGs are 1330 enzyme, 747 transcription regulator and 486 transporter genes. Importantly, cisplatin targeted 306 protein kinase genes, accounting for 59% of putative protein kinase genes in the human genome, and 92 protein phosphatase genes. This indicates that cisplatin genome-wide disturbs protein phosphorylation. This data set contains the original sequencing data of two biological replicates, matching results to the hg38 genome, and the analysis results of the distribution of the damage areas identified by the two sequencing results.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:29:23.643Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xb6s7vzcwm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xb6s7vzcwm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5wbzddvhbx","title":"Cisplatin Damaged Gene Loci ","description":"Cisplatin is a classical example of a DNA-targeted genotoxic anticancer drug, yet the exact gene loci attacked by cisplatin as well as the relationship between these gene lesions and action of the drug remain largely unexplored. In this work, we have used a forward chemical genetics method to localize and identify cisplatin damaged gene loci on the whole human genome. By combining affinity separation, high throughput sequencing and mapping to human genome (hg38), we have mapped 14012 protein-encoding genes containing platination lesions, of which 445 genes belong to 628 known gene targets for cancer therapy reported previously. Significantly, cisplatin induces lesions on 306 protein kinase genes with high fold-enrichment during our gene loci mapping, accounting for a remarkable 59% of putative human protein kinase genes. The cisplatin-damaged protein kinase genes are involved in 260 highly associated core-signaling pathways. The data deposited into this database are the mapping results of cisplatin damaged DNA for two biological replicates.     \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-26T18:58:29.8Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5wbzddvhbx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5wbzddvhbx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Anticancer Drug"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":132957,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":173,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":132957,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132229,"restricted":69,"embargoed":659},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2250},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":670},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":468},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":329},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":222}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130329},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":184},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count"